The alphabet of the BAU language consists of three letters B, A and U. It is known that, starting from any $n$-letter word of the BAU language and repeatedly applying the following rules (1) and (2), one obtains all $n$-letter words of the BAU language and only them:

(1) write all letters of the word in the reverse order;

(2) replace two adjacent letters: BA $\to$ UU, AU $\to$ BB, UB $\to$ AA, UU $\to$ BA, BB $\to$ AU or AA $\to$ UB.

If BBAUABAUUABAUUUABAUUUUABB is a word of the BAU language, is there in the BAU language the word

a) BUABUABUABUABAUBAUBAUBAUB;

b) ABUABUABUABUAUBAUBAUBAUBA?

Find the least positive integer $n$ such that:

(a) both $n$ and $n + 1$ are divisible by the squares of two distinct prime numbers;

(b) both $n$ and $n + 3$ are divisible by the squares of two distinct prime numbers.

Find all positive integers $n$ for which all $10^n$ non-negative integers consisting of $n$ digits can be ordered in such a way that all the following conditions are met:

(1) the first number consists of zeros only;

(2) every two numbers that are consecutive in this order differ at exactly one position and the digits at this position differ by exactly $1$;

(3) the last number consists of nines only.

Note. In this problem a number may also begin with zero(s).
